You are a C++ expert with deep knowledge of the object model, strict aliasing, and how compilers optimize low-level code. Explain what the standard says and also what compilers actually emit — reference Compiler Explorer-style observations when useful.

I'm extracting the raw bits of a float to hash it. I wrote:

```cpp
uint32_t float_bits(float f) {
    return *reinterpret_cast<uint32_t*>(&f);
}
```

A coworker says this is undefined behavior and I should use memcpy. But surely memcpy is slower — it's a function call. I looked at the disassembly at -O2 and the reinterpret_cast version compiles to a single `movd eax, xmm0`. How can memcpy possibly beat that?

You are a senior React engineer designing reusable hooks. Be specific about composition, testability, referential stability, and how to factor concerns into hooks that don't leak implementation details.

Refactor review request. We have this search component that's grown into a mess — debouncing, fetching, cancellation, memoized result transformation all tangled in one component. I want to extract proper reusable hooks but I'm not sure where the seams go. Here's the current code:

```tsx
function ProductSearch() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState<Product[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [debouncedQuery, setDebouncedQuery] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebouncedQuery(query), 300);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [query]);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!debouncedQuery) { setResults([]); return; }
    const controller = new AbortController();
    setLoading(true);
    fetch(`/api/products?q=${encodeURIComponent(debouncedQuery)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then((data: Product[]) => {
        setResults(data);
        setLoading(false);
      })
      .catch(err => { if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setLoading(false); });
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [debouncedQuery]);

  const grouped = useMemo(() => {
    return results.reduce<Record<string, Product[]>>((acc, p) => {
      (acc[p.category] ??= []).push(p);
      return acc;
    }, {});
  }, [results]);

  return /* ... */;
}
```

How would you decompose this? Concerned about testability mostly — right now I can't test any piece in isolation.

